  "title": "The New Colleague",
  "category": "people",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 6,
  "order": 606,
  "theme": "people",
  "culturalNote": "Describing people is where Swahili agreement really shows: adjectives take the class of the person, so \"tall\" is mrefu for one person and warefu for several — while for things Kenyans reach for the n/n form (ndefu, nzuri). It's also where Kenyan small talk lives: who's new, who has arrived, who has ever worked where. \"-wahi\" turns any verb into \"ever / never\": amewahi kufanya kazi Kampala? (has she ever worked in Kampala?).",
  "startingPoint": "Your team is getting a new member today. Your colleague Juma stops by your desk.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "adjective-agreement",
    "perfect-me",
    "perfect-negative-ja",
    "wahi-ever-never",
    "comparatives"
